  "response_text": "In the Cortex-M4, stack allocation must be done manually by the user. By design, stacks grow downwards in code, and will start at the already obtained MSP_init_val. This value is the original value of the Main Stack Pointer once the system starts up and tells you where the main stack starts. All following thread stacks will use this MSP_init_val as a reference point for later use.\\nIn order to find the PSP_val of the stack, it is important to keep in mind that the stack grows downwards, therefore the starting address of the Process Stack Pointer will be less than that of the MSP. To obtain this value, we thus need to subtract the required size of the main stack (in this case 0x400) from the initial value of our MSP. The equation:\\nPSP_val = MSP_init_val \u2013 offset\\nmust be used in order to determine our PSP_val. Without any initial location for MSP, the PSP_val does not have a reference point and thus, will not provide an accurate address for the processor.\\n",
